  "query_preview": "..\n\nToday through Sunday...\n\nA shortwave trough is moving through the region tonight, ahead of\nan approaching upper low. After good parameters for thunderstorms\nlargely went to waste Sunday afternoon and evening, the shortwave\nwill bring enough ascent for activity to begin overnight.\nScattered showers and thunderstorms are forecast to develop and\nmove to the northeast into Monday morning. The greatest chance for\nprecipitation exists across south-central Montana (where there is\nup to a 70 percent chance of precipitation) through this morning.\nThe chance for precipitation has increased a bit with recent model\nruns over south-eastern Montana and northern Wyoming. The latest\nchance is generally 20 to 40%.\n\nA few storms may become strong to severe overnight, as favorable\nparameters will still exist over the region. The primary threats\nwill be strong wind gusts, hail, and heavy rainfall. Currently, \nthe SPC has a Marginal Risk (Level 1 out of 5) of severe \nthunderstorms. Through the morning hours the chance for\nprecipitation will decrease along with the risk for strong to\nsevere thunderstorms.\n\nThe chance for isolated showers and thunderstorms will begin to \nincrease later this morning and last into the early afternoon, as \nthe main low begins to move through Montana. With the low moving\nto the northeast through the afternoon, drier air looks to move \ninto the forecast area mitigating the chance for precipitation. \nHowever, westerly winds will increase across the region, with \ngusts in the range of 20 to 40 mph, with locally higher gusts in \nthe mountains and foothills west of Billings. There is a moderate \nchance (40 to 60 percent) for gusts up to 50 mph in the Livingston\nto Big Timber to Harlowton areas.\n\nWindy conditions will continue into Tuesday, though the strongest\nwinds will be more likely in the far east and along the Dakotas\nBorder. The latest probability for gusts of 50 mph is around 25%\nfor Baker. The drying conditions along with the winds will bring \nareas of elevated fire weather conditions today and Tuesday.\n\nThe overall pattern becomes warmer and drier through mid-week.\nThursday could see increased fire weather concerns, with an \napproaching cold front, highs above normal, and minimum relative \nhumidity values in the teens percent. This will be worth \nmonitoring as the week progresses.\n\nLooking towards the weekend, models are depicting modest \nagreement in another system bringing cooler and wetter conditions.\nUncertainty remains though so continue to monitor the forecast.\nPM/LA",
